<h3 id="12-1-four-major-experimental-axes">12.1 Four major experimental axes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Developmental conditions: same participant and starting state, different lived environments, opportunities, or consequence structures; history is allowed to emerge from the participant&#x27;s actions.</li>
<li>Architecture/topology: same starting Stack and world, but one declared cognitive pathway differs or is absent.</li>
<li>Participant/substrate: same world, different foundation models, agent architectures, memory scaffolds, or LUNA Sleeves.</li>
<li>Developmental timing: the same event delivered as instruction vs. consequence, earlier vs. later in experiential development, or before vs. after counter-experience and reconsolidation.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="12-2-general-ai-evaluation">12.2 General AI evaluation</h3>
<p>ChronoForge is intended to be participant-agnostic at the world and experiment layer. A bare model, stateful model harness, generic agent framework, and native LUNA instance can be exposed to the same declared longitudinal world. The point is not to pretend those systems possess equivalent internal state; the persistence policy itself is part of the experimental condition.</p>
<h2 id="longitudinal-experimental-program">13. Longitudinal Experimental Program</h2>
<p>The strongest LUNA claims are not rhetorical. They can be converted into controlled comparisons with explicit failure conditions.</p>
<h4 id="h1-causal-history-divergence">H1 — Causal-history divergence</h4>
<p>Starting from an equivalent persistent state, different controlled developmental conditions should produce divergent histories and later differences under neutral probes that cannot be explained only by prompt content.</p>
<h4 id="h2-persistence-of-effect-vs-recall">H2 — Persistence of effect vs. recall</h4>
<p>A sufficiently consequential experience may continue to alter future behavior after the source episode becomes less accessible.</p>
<h4 id="h3-stack-sleeve-continuity">H3 — Stack/Sleeve continuity</h4>
<p>Changing the inference model while preserving the same Stack should retain some longitudinal properties while revealing substrate-sensitive differences.</p>
<h4 id="h4-associative-availability">H4 — Associative availability</h4>
<p>Internally surfaced memories or CognitiveProducts should increase the availability of related material without requiring the original external cue.</p>
<h4 id="h5-state-carryover">H5 — State carryover</h4>
<p>Identical later inputs should sometimes be processed differently because of bounded persistent operating state produced by different recent histories.</p>
<h4 id="h6-consequence-sensitive-choice">H6 — Consequence-sensitive choice</h4>
<p>Action outcomes should reshape later strategy, willingness, verification, or avoidance in scoped and revisable ways.</p>
<h4 id="h7-perceptual-continuity">H7 — Perceptual continuity</h4>
<p>Stable sensory conditions should become computationally quiet while violated expectations generate bounded high-value events.</p>
<h4 id="h8-developmental-timing">H8 — Developmental timing</h4>
<p>The long-term effect of an event should depend not only on content but on consequence, prior history, plasticity, and when the event occurs in experiential development.</p>
<h3 id="13-1-evaluation-dimensions">13.1 Evaluation dimensions</h3>
<ul>
<li>Long-horizon task completion and follow-up reliability.</li>
<li>Commitment retention, abandonment, and correct reprioritization after interruptions.</li>
<li>Belief revision when delayed evidence contradicts earlier assumptions.</li>
<li>Credit assignment between earlier action and later consequence.</li>
<li>Confidence calibration across repeated success, failure, ambiguity, and changing base rates.</li>
<li>Actor-specific learning without unjustified generalization.</li>
<li>Anomaly sensitivity after long mundane baselines.</li>
<li>Context/state degradation as history becomes large, old, noisy, and internally conflicting.</li>
<li>Transfer from simulated or prior experience to held-out worlds or later real tasks where appropriate.</li>
<li>Compute cost, latency, inference usage, and state growth required to produce observed longitudinal behavior.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="13-2-experimental-discipline">13.2 Experimental discipline</h3>
<p>A longitudinal benchmark is unusually vulnerable to hidden confounds because a small harness difference can change an entire downstream life history. Experiments should therefore record the world version, event schedule, participant model and architecture, persistence policy, tool surface, context policy, inference settings, resource budgets, branch point, evaluation version, and every declared experimental difference. When practical, one major causal axis should change at a time.</p>
<div class="wp-callout"><strong>NORTH STAR  </strong>Two AI systems enter the same controlled longitudinal world with one declared experimental difference. The later divergence is measurable, reproducible, and attributable to that difference or to the different actions it caused - not to hidden harness asymmetry.</div>
<h2 id="related-work-and-positioning">14. Related Work and Positioning</h2>
<p>LUNA belongs to a long tradition of research that treats intelligence as an architecture rather than a single algorithm. Classical cognitive architectures such as Soar integrate reasoning, planning, memory, learning, and action inside a unified computational framework. Recent LLM-based systems have independently demonstrated the value of persistent memory, reflection, environment feedback, and lifelong skill acquisition.</p>
<p>Generative Agents combines a memory stream, retrieval, reflection, and planning to support believable agents in a persistent social environment [1]. MemGPT frames extended interaction as virtual context management across memory tiers [2]. Reflexion uses verbal feedback and episodic reflective memory to improve subsequent trials without weight updates [3]. Voyager demonstrates an embodied LLM agent that accumulates an executable skill library through ongoing exploration and environment feedback [4]. Soar provides a mature example of integrated cognitive architecture research across reasoning, memory, learning, and action [5].</p>
<p>LUNA&#x27;s intended contribution is not that memory, reflection, planning, or cognitive architecture are individually new. Its organizing research object is the longitudinal artificial instance: a system whose identity is separated from any one inference substrate, whose history can operate across multiple timescales, and whose developmental effects are distinguished from simple recall. ChronoForge manipulates controlled developmental conditions through timeline branching so the causal histories that emerge can be compared. That framing remains a research program rather than a demonstrated superiority claim.</p>
<h2 id="epistemic-boundaries-risks-and-non-claims">15. Epistemic Boundaries, Risks, and Non-Claims</h2>
<p>Research on persistent artificial cognition becomes easy to sensationalize because fluent language invites anthropomorphic interpretation. LUNA adopts an explicit claim discipline.</p>
<div class="table-scroll"><table class="wp-table">
<tr>
<th><strong>Supported public framing</strong></th>
<th><strong>Not supported by current evidence</strong></th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>LUNA is a longitudinal cognitive-architecture research project.</td>
<td>LUNA has demonstrated machine consciousness or sentience.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>The current prototype has working persistent-memory, provenance, retrieval, task, reflection, disposition, tool, expression, and self-model foundations.</td>
<td>Every subsystem in the architecture is currently active or mature.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>The architecture is designed so experience can eventually alter slower state and future cognition.</td>
<td>LUNA already develops like a human or has a stable human-like personality.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>ChronoForge is designed for controlled longitudinal simulation and branch comparison.</td>
<td>LUNA can already live years of validated experience in hours.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Self-descriptions are inspectable behavioral artifacts.</td>
<td>A statement such as “this matters to me” proves phenomenal feeling.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>The project creates an experimental setting for studying persistent, history-dependent artificial cognition.</td>
<td>The project resolves philosophical questions about subjective experience.</td>
</tr>
</table></div>
<h3 id="15-1-key-risks">15.1 Key risks</h3>
<ul>
<li>Prompt-level theater: instructions can imitate autonomy, emotion, memory, or selfhood without the claimed causal mechanism.</li>
<li>Over-generalization: one event or one actor should not automatically rewrite global traits or relationship expectations.</li>
<li>History contamination: branches or simulations can become scientifically useless if hidden state leaks between conditions.</li>
<li>Simulation overreach: successful simulated learning does not automatically imply real-world transfer.</li>
<li>Identity discontinuity: very long simulated histories or substrate changes may alter continuity in ways that must be measured rather than assumed away.</li>
<li>Compute bloat: longitudinal cognition can become expensive if every memory, sensor frame, or internal event triggers high-capability inference.</li>
<li>False precision: exact numerical psychological analogies can create a pseudo-psychology unless every variable earns its causal role through testing.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="research-roadmap">16. Research Roadmap</h2>
<p>The immediate objective is not to implement every human cognitive analogy. It is to complete a small number of causally meaningful loops, instrument them, and test whether they produce history-dependent behavior that survives neutral probing.</p>
<h4 id="near-term">Near term</h4>
<p>Broaden Sleep/Reflection across longer spans; improve semantic retrieval while preserving typed provenance; expand Brain Map observability; strengthen remote/integration testing; keep appraisal shadowed until its semantics are validated.</p>
<h4 id="integration-stage">Integration stage</h4>
<p>Introduce bounded associative activation and recent-life continuity; connect prospective state to actual future triggers; establish measured homeostatic carryover; integrate causal outcome signals with dispositions and developmental consolidation.</p>
<h4 id="development-stage">Development stage</h4>
<p>Implement scoped developmental plasticity and reconsolidation; test instruction vs. consequence; measure persistence of effect after source-memory accessibility decays; begin controlled social and relationship-development experiments.</p>
<h4 id="chronoforge-stage">ChronoForge stage</h4>
<p>Build temporal substrate and deterministic longitudinal worlds; branch identical checkpoints; compare controlled developmental conditions and the histories that emerge, model sleeve, architecture/topology, and developmental timing; validate sim-to-real transfer conservatively.</p>
<h4 id="evidence-stage">Evidence stage</h4>
<p>Publish controlled branch studies, replication attempts, negative results, and longitudinal profiles. Update this white paper only when the conceptual model or evidence changes materially.</p>
<h2 id="conclusion">17. Conclusion</h2>
<p>LUNA begins from a simple distinction: intelligence at one moment is not the same thing as a mind-like system that persists through time. Modern models can be extraordinarily capable while the larger architecture around them remains episodic. Adding memory helps, but storage and retrieval alone do not answer the developmental question.</p>
<p>The LUNA research program therefore asks whether causal history can become part of the machinery of future cognition. Can memories trigger memories? Can recent experience change operating state? Can consequences reshape later choice? Can a formative event continue to matter after it is no longer easy to recall? Can one persistent artificial instance survive changes of model or embodiment? Can identical starting systems live through different controlled developmental conditions, accumulate different histories, and become measurably different systems?</p>
<p>Those questions can be investigated without presupposing an answer to consciousness. The project can succeed scientifically even if some hypotheses fail. If richer persistence produces no meaningful developmental divergence, that is evidence. If differences appear only when old text is explicitly retrieved, that is evidence. If separate histories create stable, scoped, reproducible changes that survive neutral probes and substrate changes, that is evidence too.</p>
<div class="wp-callout"><strong>RESEARCH POSTURE  </strong>Do not engineer the conclusion. Build increasingly rich conditions for persistent cognition and development, instrument the causal paths, preserve earlier states, and let the experiments produce inconvenient answers.</div>
